complete -c netops -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a ping -d 'Monitor round-trip time and loss to an address from this collector.'
complete -c netops -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a subscribe -d 'Mark a link as one you pay for, and record what it is sold at. Marking it is what puts it on the overview as its own tile and includes it in the WAN total; the rate itself is kept as a soft limit -- nothing is enforced, and nothing is discarded for exceeding it. The tiles read in bits per second rather than as a share of the contract, so a wrong figure here costs you the soft limit and not the reading. --facing says which way the port looks: isp (default) is the port that reaches the provider, where what the switch receives is your download; lan is the port facing inward, where it is the other way round. --clear removes the subscription.'
complete -c netops -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a name -d 'Give a monitored port a name of your own for dashboards -- "AFRIX" rather than Gi1/0/16 -- and optionally pin its colour with --color, so it is the same colour on every chart instead of whatever the grapher assigns by arrival order. Colours: teal, orange, green, violet, or an explicit #rrggbb. Those four were checked together for separation, lightness, and colour-blind readability; a fifth of your own was not. The name is not part of a series identity, so naming a port that has been collected for weeks keeps its history rather than starting a new series. Everything after the target is the name, so it needs no quoting to contain spaces. --clear removes it and the port shows as the device calls it again.'
complete -c netops -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a push -d 'Send this collectors current readings to a VictoriaMetrics import endpoint. They arrive as the counters they are, carrying this collectors id, so they stay distinguishable from the receiving machines own readings and every dashboard reads them unchanged. This is the other way a poller hands its readings over: pulling fetches them over SSH and needs a path inward, pushing needs a path outward, and a network usually permits one or the other. The receiving machine must have been told to accept it -- storage binds to loopback until setup local --accept-push --confirm says otherwise.'
complete -c netops -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a dashboard -d 'Print every dashboards URL and say whether the grapher is answering. save keeps a dashboard you have rearranged in the browser, writing it in place of the generated one so it survives the next stack init; revert goes back to the generated version. Dashboards are generated because a generator keeps five of them consistent and hand-edited JSON does not, but a board you have arranged the way you read it should not have to be given up for that. --open launches the overview in a browser. When it is not answering this says which of the two reasons it is -- the service is not started, or the machines per-user systemd manager is not running at all -- because from a browser those look identical and lead somewhere quite different.'
complete -c netops -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a health -d 'Show switch-level readings for every switch with a watched port: uptime, control-plane CPU, memory, temperature, and open sessions. These are collected automatically alongside the ports, because a port that went quiet reads differently depending on whether its switch rebooted or ran out of CPU. A dash means the switch does not answer that OID; nothing is wrong. Failed SSH logins are in no standard MIB -- read the switchs syslog for those.'
