complete -c netop -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a backfill -d 'Import history collected before the metrics stack existed into VictoriaMetrics. Stored rates are published as netrune_history_* gauges, kept separate from the live counter series so a derived value is never read as a measured one.'
complete -c netop -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a stack -d 'Write the VictoriaMetrics scrape config, vmalert rules, dashboards for the selected grapher, systemd user units, and a compose file. Dashboards are provisioned from these files rather than edited in a browser. graphers lists which are available. --role collector generates the collector alone -- no exposition, no storage, no dashboards -- which is what a remote poller needs: the machine that pulls from it keeps and draws the readings, and anything published on the poller would be a new listener on the network it was placed there to observe.'
complete -c netop -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a export -d 'Print this collectors stored readings as JSONL for another install to pull. Read-only, and limited to what this collector measured: a poller does not pass on what it pulled from somewhere else.'
complete -c netop -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a pull -d 'Merge a pollers readings into this install. --remote fetches them over SSH; --file merges an export the poller wrote, which is what to use where SSH between the two subnets is not permitted -- an export is a file, and anything that can move a file will do. - reads standard input. A file is merged whole rather than by window, and merging is idempotent, so re-reading one adds nothing. Without --since a fetch resumes from the newest reading already merged, so a repeated pull carries only what is new.'
complete -c netop -n '__fish_seen_subcommand_from traffic' -a remote -d 'Manage remote pollers, and run any traffic command on one over SSH. Anything that is not add, list, or remove is forwarded verbatim to the real CLI on the poller, so remote control and local control cannot drift apart.'
